{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>For the people come to me to inquire of Elohim.</b> He did not say 'to inquire of YHWH,' because he was occupied with the matter of judgment, and therefore he said 'Elohim,' for judgment belongs to Elohim. And so he says, ״והודעתי את חקי האלהים ואת תורותיו״ — 'and I make known the statutes of Elohim and His laws' — and he did not say 'the statutes of YHWH.' And the explanation of the verse is: they must stand before me a long time during the day, because they ask me many matters. Some of them come to me so that I may pray for their sick, and some of them come to me so that I may inform them what has been lost from them. And so we find that inquiry of Elohim occurs in both these matters — as he said (II Kings 8:8): ״ודרשת את ה' מאותו לאמר האחיה מחלי זה״ — that he should pray for him and inform him whether his prayer has been heard — and as is said of Rebekah (Genesis 25:22): ״ותלך לדרוש את ה'״; and we find the matter of a lost object similarly in Israel (I Samuel 9:9): ״כה אמר האיש בלכתו לדרוש אלהים לכו ונלכה עד הרואה״. And furthermore, I judge them when they have a case — it comes to me and I judge; and furthermore, I teach them Torah, as it says, ״והודעתי את חקי האלהים״ וגו׳."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:45.870534672Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}